Subject: [PATCH] Apply factory defaults when the config fails to parse (C1-durable)
Target: prudynt-t @ f4b32289359d176fb0795d9f3735af0bd9d6d2fd (branch stable).
Stacks on: 0001-0011. Applies with git apply -p1 / patch -p1 AFTER them. Touches only
Config.cpp, in regions no other patch modifies. Only the parse-FAILURE path changes; a normal
load is unaffected.

Durable defense-in-depth for the config-corruption brick (audit C1; the rootfs S31prudynt
guard is the first line, this is the binary backstop for when that guard cannot restore -
e.g. a full overlay). If /etc/prudynt.json is truncated/corrupt, load_config() returns null,
readConfig() returns false, and CFG::load() was skipping the entire item-processing block
(if (jsonConfig) { ... }). With the block skipped, NO item got its items-table default, so
every field kept its C++ zero-initialised value - critically image.isp_bypass = false (no
in-class initializer), which disables ISP bypass and BREAKS streaming on this sc2336p unit
(plus stream/rtsp enable flags falling to false). A permanent dead stream the OPTIONS
watchdog cannot see.

Fix (two surgical changes, parse-failure path only):
  1. handleConfigItem() no longer early-returns on a null jsonConfig. getNestedValue() is
     null-safe (returns null on a null root), so each item simply finds no JSON value and
     falls through to the EXISTING default-assignment logic (item.value = item.defaultValue,
     with strdup for char*), and sensor params still read their /proc value.
  2. CFG::load() runs the item loops unconditionally (bare scope instead of if (jsonConfig)),
     so those defaults are actually applied when the config didn't parse.
Result: a corrupt/truncated/missing config degrades to the FACTORY-SAFE configuration (the
items-table defaults: isp_bypass=true, streams/rtsp enabled, valid codec/geometry...) and the
stream comes up, instead of a zero-initialised brick. A normal load (non-null jsonConfig) is
byte-for-byte unchanged. No new state. handleConfigItem2() (the write path used by
updateConfig()) is intentionally untouched - its null guard is correct for writes.

diff --git a/src/Config.cpp b/src/Config.cpp
index 1314d29..4b53637 100644
--- a/src/Config.cpp
+++ b/src/Config.cpp
@@ -995,8 +995,12 @@ void handleConfigItem(JsonValue *jsonConfig, ConfigItem<T> &item) {
   bool readFromProc = false;
   bool readFromConfig = false;
 
-  if (!jsonConfig)
-    return;
+  // C1-durable: do NOT early-return on a null jsonConfig (corrupt / truncated /
+  // missing config). getNestedValue() below is null-safe (returns null), so each
+  // item falls through to its items-table default - and to its /proc value for
+  // sensor params. This degrades a parse failure to a factory-safe configuration
+  // (e.g. image.isp_bypass stays true) instead of leaving every field
+  // zero-initialised (isp_bypass -> false breaks streaming on this sensor).
 
   // For sensor parameters with proc paths, prioritize proc files over JSON
   if (isSensorProcParameter(item)) {
@@ -1333,7 +1337,14 @@ void CFG::load() {
   config_loaded = readConfig();
   LOG_DEBUG("CFG::load() - Read config, loaded=" << config_loaded);
 
-  if (jsonConfig) {
+  // C1-durable: always run the item loops, even when jsonConfig is null (a parse
+  // failure / corrupt or truncated config). handleConfigItem() now substitutes
+  // each item's items-table default on a null/missing value, so the config
+  // degrades to factory-safe instead of zero-initialised fields. Without this the
+  // whole block was skipped on a parse failure and image.isp_bypass fell back to
+  // false -> ISP bypass disabled -> dead stream. (Bare scope; the close brace
+  // below is unchanged.)
+  {
     LOG_DEBUG("CFG::load() - Processing config items");
     LOG_DEBUG("CFG::load() - Processing bool items (" << boolItems.size()
                                                       << ")");
